<%!-- ============================================================
  Containers — the runner in a container. The honest core is
  namespace visibility (a runner acts on what its namespace can
  reach). The official image (ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner,
  published per runner release by runner-release.yml) covers the
  container observation set; extension is FROM + tools + emisar
  pack install. State persistence: cloud.token_path + the dispatch log;
  external identity follows the hostname unless runner.id is pinned. EMISAR_GROUP /
  EMISAR_RUNNER_ID env overrides relabel without a config mount. Kubernetes
  (DaemonSet) and Nomad (system job) build on this page. Facts
  verified against runner/release/Dockerfile, install.sh, and
  runner/internal/config.
============================================================ --%>
<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="containers"
      updated="September 3, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"what-it-sees", "What a containerized runner sees"},
        {"official-image", "The official image"},
        {"building-blocks", "Identity and state"},
        {"extending", "Extend it for your packs"},
        {"sidecar", "A sidecar next to one app"},
        {"orchestrators", "One per node: Kubernetes and Nomad"},
        {"next", "After it is connected"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="containers">
        <:dek>
          The runner is a plain foreground process, so it runs in a container. The container
          changes what the runner can see, not whether it runs.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        To install straight onto a machine instead, see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/host-install"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Linux host</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>A host with a container runtime. The examples use Docker.</:item>
        <:item>
          Outbound HTTPS from the container to <code class="text-zinc-300">emisar.dev:443</code>
          for the control plane, plus <code class="text-zinc-300">registry.emisar.dev:443</code>
          for pack installs and updates. Nothing listens inbound, so there is no port to publish.
          See
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/network-requirements"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >Network requirements</.link>
          for the optional GitHub fallback domains.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Take the enrollment key from <a
          href={~p"/app/runners/install"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners → Connect a runner</a>. Replace <code class="font-mono">X.Y.Z</code>
        with the current version from the <.external_link
          href="https://emisar.dev/releases/runner/latest.json"
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >runner release manifest</.external_link>, then start the container:
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="docker host"
        copy_text="docker run -d --name emisar -v emisar-data:/var/lib/emisar -v emisar-logs:/var/log/emisar -e EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY=emkey-enroll-… ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner:X.Y.Z"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> docker run -d --name emisar \
    -v emisar-data:/var/lib/emisar \
    -v emisar-logs:/var/log/emisar \
    -e EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY=emkey-enroll-… \
    ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner:X.Y.Z
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> docker logs -f emisar</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The first volume keeps the runner's identity token; the second keeps the
        hash-chained security journal at <code class="font-mono">/var/log/emisar</code>, so both survive replacing
        the container.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        In the console, open
        <a
          href={~p"/app/runners"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners</a>
        — the container appears online, with the actions its packs advertise.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="what-it-sees">What a containerized runner sees</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A runner acts only on resources available in its namespace. On a VM or bare metal, that
        namespace covers the whole machine. Most packs target hosts, and some need systemd —
        that is why the
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/host-install"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          host install
        </.link>
        is the default. In a bare container, those packs see only the container's processes,
        filesystem, and network.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The same limit is the control. A sidecar can join one app's process and network
        namespaces and mount selected volumes — it then reaches every process, interface, and
        endpoint in them. You choose whether the agent operates one app, one node, or every node.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="official-image">The official image</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Every runner release publishes
        <code class="font-mono">ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner</code>
        for linux/amd64 and linux/arm64. The registry has no <code class="font-mono">latest</code>
        tag — you always deploy a version you named. What is in the image is part of what the
        runner can do, so treat an image change with the same care as trusting a new pack.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The image contains the release-built runner binary and three packs that cover what a
        container can see: <code class="font-mono">fs-search</code>
        for its filesystem, <code class="font-mono">debugging</code>
        for its processes, and <code class="font-mono">network-tls</code>
        for its network — plus only the tools those packs call.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        It runs as the non-root user <code class="font-mono">65532</code>, connects to
        <code class="font-mono">emisar.dev</code>
        (set <code class="font-mono">EMISAR_URL</code>
        for a self-hosted control plane), reads <code class="font-mono">EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY</code>, and keeps state under <code class="font-mono">/var/lib/emisar</code>. The baked config admits only low-risk
        actions, so the default image can observe but not fix — mount your own config to allow
        the debugging pack's fix actions.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each release notes its image digest, and the image carries signed build provenance and an
        SBOM:
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="shell"
        copy_text="gh attestation verify oci://ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner:X.Y.Z --repo andrewdryga/emisar --signer-workflow AndrewDryga/emisar/.github/workflows/runner-release-trusted.yml --source-ref refs/tags/runner-vX.Y.Z --deny-self-hosted-runners"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> gh attestation verify oci://ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner:X.Y.Z \
    --repo andrewdryga/emisar \
    --signer-workflow AndrewDryga/emisar/.github/workflows/runner-release-trusted.yml \
    --source-ref refs/tags/runner-vX.Y.Z \
    --deny-self-hosted-runners</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="building-blocks">Identity and state</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        By default, the runner uses the container hostname as its identity. After the first connect,
        the per-runner token lands at <code class="font-mono">cloud.token_path</code>
        (<code class="font-mono">/var/lib/emisar/token.json</code>
        in the official image). Keep the hostname stable and
        <code class="font-mono">/var/lib/emisar</code>
        in a volume so restarts preserve the same runner and its durable dispatch state.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The other model is to keep nothing, the way
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/autoscaling-fleets"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >autoscaling fleets</.link>
        treat hosts: no volume, a reusable enrollment key in the template, and a unique hostname
        per container — Docker's default, the container ID, already is one. Each replacement then
        registers as a new runner, and the old record sits offline until <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/autoscaling-fleets" <> "#cleanup"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >retention removes it</.link>. The unique hostname is required — two containers sharing
        one hostname register as the SAME runner and fight over its connection — and dispatch
        state is not preserved: a replaced container abandons whatever it was running.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Two environment variables relabel a fleet without a config file.
        <code class="font-mono">EMISAR_GROUP</code>
        sets <code class="font-mono">runner.group</code>. The image uses
        <code class="font-mono">containers</code>
        by default. Actions and runbooks can target a
        whole
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet" <> "#groups-labels"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >group</.link>
        at once, so give each fleet its own group instead of pooling unrelated fleets.
        <code class="font-mono">EMISAR_RUNNER_ID</code>
        pins <code class="font-mono">runner.id</code>
        when rescheduling changes the container hostname but it is still the same runner. For labels or admission settings, mount your own file over <code class="font-mono">/etc/emisar/config.yaml</code>:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="config.yaml"><span class="text-sky-300">schema_version</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">1</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">runner</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">id</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"payments-sidecar"</span>
  <span class="text-sky-300">group</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"sidecars"</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">paths</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">data_dir</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"/var/lib/emisar"</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">events</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">jsonl_path</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"/var/log/emisar/events.jsonl"</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">cloud</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">enrollment_key_env</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY"</span>
  <span class="text-sky-300">token_path</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"/var/lib/emisar/token.json"</span></.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The key you started with is single-use — right for one container, wrong for a template
        that scales. For DaemonSets and system jobs,
        mint a
        <a
          href={~p"/app/runners/keys/new"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >reusable enrollment key</a>
        (optionally capped at a number of uses) and hand that to the template. See
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet" <> "#enrollment-keys"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >enrollment keys</.link>
        for the lifecycle details.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="extending">Extend it for your packs</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Actions run inside the container, so a pack's client must be in the image too — the
        <code class="font-mono">postgres</code>
        pack needs <code class="font-mono">psql</code>
        there. Extend the official image with the tools your packs call, then install the packs:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Dockerfile">FROM ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner:X.Y.Z
USER root
RUN apt-get update \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends postgresql-client \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
    && emisar pack install postgres=X.Y.Z --hash sha256:…
USER 65532</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Pin the pack version and content hash — the pack's page in the
        <.link navigate={~p"/packs"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          pack catalog
        </.link>
        shows both, with the install command ready to copy — so the image build is reproducible
        and a tampered registry copy is rejected. Keep the additions
        as narrow as the workload allows.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Building your own image from scratch instead — a different distro, a hardened base?
        Install the release binary with <code class="font-mono text-brand-300">--no-service</code>: it skips init detection, the
        service user, and the systemd unit, and just puts the binary in place. Supervision belongs
        to whatever runs the container: <code class="font-mono">docker run --restart</code>, a
        Kubernetes restart policy, or the Nomad client.
      </p>
      <.docs_release_verifier_prerequisite />
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="shell"
        copy_text="curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh | sudo bash -s -- --no-service"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh | sudo bash -s -- --no-service</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="sidecar">A sidecar next to one app</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        If the agent operates one app, run the runner as a second container beside it. Join the
        app's PID namespace, network, and read-only volumes; the runner then sees the app's
        processes and files, and reaches every destination that network can. It cannot see the
        host's processes or filesystem unless you grant them. Policy, approvals, and audit work
        unchanged.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> docker run -d --restart unless-stopped \
    --name emisar-payments \
    --pid container:payments \
    --network container:payments \
    --volumes-from payments:ro \
    -e EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY=emkey-enroll-… \
    -v /etc/emisar/config.yaml:/etc/emisar/config.yaml:ro \
    -v emisar-payments-data:/var/lib/emisar \
    -v emisar-payments-logs:/var/log/emisar \
    ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner:X.Y.Z</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The official image already covers the core sidecar work: reading the app's files,
        processes, and network state. The command above adds an operator-owned config and
        persistent token and journal volumes. Name the container, the volumes, and the config's
        <code class="font-mono">runner.id</code>
        after the app — <code class="font-mono">runner.id</code>
        is also the name the console shows, and the sidecar shares the app container's
        hostname, so a second sidecar on the same host would otherwise collide on all three. If the agent needs the app's CLI,
        <a
          href="#extending"
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >extend the image</a>
        with that client.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="orchestrators">One per node: Kubernetes and Nomad</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        To operate nodes instead of one app, run one runner per node — a Kubernetes DaemonSet
        or a Nomad system job. Both need an image, a persistent state volume, and a reusable
        enrollment key. Each guide provides a complete manifest:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <.link navigate={~p"/docs/kubernetes"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Kubernetes DaemonSet</strong>
            </.link>
            — the pod spec decides exactly what each node's runner can see: <code class="font-mono">hostPID</code>, <code class="font-mono">hostNetwork</code>, and read-only host mounts.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <.link navigate={~p"/docs/nomad"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Nomad system job</strong>
            </.link>
            — one runner on every client node; an artifact stanza fetches the release binary, and
            a host volume keeps the token and dispatch state.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <.docs_h2 id="next">After it is connected</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          Manage the runner fleet
        </.link>
        covers work after the first connection — groups, labels, pack credentials, updates,
        reconnects, and removal. To roll a container fleet out in phases, see the <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/production"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Go to production</.link>.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
